"],"source_records":["bwb:9788867494132","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-031.mrc:361912824:1214","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_045.bib.mrc:92317253:2702"],"title":"Francesco Clemente","weight":"0.341","pagination":"96","subtitle":"Watchtowers, Keys, Threads, Gates","full_title":"Francesco Clemente Watchtowers, Keys, Threads, Gates","works":[{"key":"/works/OL4477279W"}],"key":"/books/OL34981208M","lc_classifications":["N6923.C54 A4 2020","ND"],"oclc_numbers":["1226397015"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"I?m a painter by nature, almost biologically, but I?m also a painter by default, culturally, because I found out that a lot of what I want to convey to the world can only be told through image and not through words.\"?Francesco Clemente0This richly illustrated volume documents 'Watchtowers, Keys, Threads, Gates', Francesco Clemente?s exhibition at Dallas Contemporary in 2019, curated by Peter Doroshenko. The large-scale installation presented there included a massive, site-specific fresco and two series of sculptures realized in the artist?s signature style. The overall dreamlike atmosphere was firmly in keeping with Clemente?s aesthetics and imaginary.0Through the winding waves murals??realized with the help of three Oaxacan artists??and the bodies of the sculptures??created over the past five years in collaboration with artisans in India??Clemente constructed a labyrinth of patterns and resonances made up of the elements enumerated in the title. Visitors entered his mythological universe and experienced full immersion in his ongoing research on gesture, knowledge, transition, and color.00Exhibition: Dallas Contemporary, Dallas, USA (13.04-18.08.2019)."},"number_of_pages":96,"latest_revision":3,"revision":3,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2021-10-09T17:10:08.460338"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-07-26T16:01:10.001789"}}},"ISBN:9780876330845":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9780876330845","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL1884729M/Francesco_Clemente","preview":"borrow","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/francescoclement0000perc","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/12790336-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Philadelphia Museum of Art","Distributed by Rizzoli","Rizzoli International Publications, Incorporated"],"number_of_pages":184,"subtitle":"three worlds","isbn_10":["0876330847","0847812979"],"lc_classifications":["NC257.C575 A4 1990","NC257.C575A4 1990"],"key":"/books/OL1884729M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL893335A","name":"Ann Percy"}],"publish_places":["Philadelphia, Pa","New York"],"contributions":["Clemente, Francesco, 1952-","Foye, Raymond, 1957-","Kramrisch, Stella, 1898-","Sottsass, Ettore, 1917-","Philadelphia Museum of Art."],"pagination":"184, [1] p. :","source_records":["marc:marc_records_scriblio_net/part21.dat:88695204:1339","marc:marc_loc_updates/v36.i01.records.utf8:2991404:1348","marc:marc_cca/b10621386.out:22509497:1315","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part20.utf8:63109322:1348","ia:francescoclement0000perc","promise:bwb_daily_pallets_2022-03-17","bwb:9780847812974","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-002.mrc:500321083:3796","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/ab.bib.02.20150123.full.mrc:96271885:1530","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_032.bib.mrc:92183839:4285"],"title":"Francesco Clemente","dewey_decimal_class":["709/.2"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references (p. [185]) and index.\nCatalogue of an exhibition held at the Philadelphia Museum of Art, Oct. 20-Dec. 23, 1990; Wadsworth Atheneum, Hartford, Jan. 27-March 17, 1991; San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, April 11-June 2, 1991; Virginia Museum of Fine Arts, Richmond, July 2-August 18, 1991."},"identifiers":{"librarything":["12895"],"goodreads":["1494524","6172253"]},"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"lccn":["90045673"],"subjects":["Clemente, Francesco, 1952-  -- Exhibitions"],"publish_date":"1990","publish_country":"pau","by_statement":"Ann Percy and Raymond Foye ; with essays by Stella Kramrisch and Ettore Sottsass ; organized by Ann Percy.","works":[{"key":"/works/OL4477279W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"covers":[12790336],"ocaid":"francescoclement0000perc","local_id":["urn:bwbsku:W7-AJF-244"],"oclc_numbers":["22306068"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Francesco Clemente: Three Worlds brings together more than 100 works on paper--watercolors, pastels, gouaches, folding screens, and books--by the contemporary Italian artist Francesco Clemente. The exhibition reflects Clemente's interest in the cultures of Italy (where he was born), India (where he visits each year), and New York (where he now resides). Each of these \"worlds\" possesses its own sensibility. While working in Italy, Clemente is affected by the artistic genius of its ancient past. The mystical traditions of India and its cultural, racial, and religious diversity also attract him. In 1982, Clemente moved to New York, where he finds the cosmopolitan aspect of the city and its unique blend of \"high\" and \"low\" culture exhilarating. Known for his highly inventive, personal imagery often presented in a dreamlike, surrealist style, Clemente enjoys an international reputation as one of the most provocative and gifted young Italian painters. His superb command of draftsmanship and sumptuous use of color make his images compelling, however the personal nature of many of these images also lend them an alien, irrational aura. In many ways, the viewer cannot explain or understand Clemente's imagery, but is simply left to behold."},"latest_revision":15,"revision":15,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-02-14T19:18:55.126105"}}},"ISBN:9783869842257":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9783869842257","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL28760635M/Francesco_Clemente","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http:/